W H6, B D5, W D6, B C5, W B5, B B4, W J6, B B6 (cut: B5 can't escape), W F6, B F5 (my right
group had only 2 libs E4/F5), W B7, B A5 (captures B5), W A7, B B5, W C7, B A6, W pass,
B H1!! (seki-breaker, see below), W pass, B J2 (captures 9), pass pass.
Final: Black rows 1-5 + A6/B6 = 47. White rows 7-9 + C6-J6 = 34.
Takeaways:
- Opening C4 + F4 + E5 (low corner, then 4th-line extension, then row-5 center) split the board
  rows 1-5 for me vs 6-9 for White. That 45-vs-36 split is a WIN for Black — just hold row 5.
- Invasion G3 under my F4: G4 (cutting it from its friend G6) + H5 + G5 sealed it; then block
  each crawl (E3), hane F2, atari E1, and J4 (outside reduction) left it a 4-point square
  -> dead. Reading before J4 was correct: after W J3 atari, J5 connect, square four = dead.
- SEKI TRAP: after B H2 (vital point), the white group and my H2 shared exactly 2 libs (H1,J2)
  and nobody else had libs => SEKI, not dead! Passing would have lost the game (white's 9 stones
  would count). Fix: sacrifice by filling one shared lib (H1) -> if White captures 2 stones the
  space is a 2-point eye = dead; White passed so J2 captured all. ALWAYS check: does the
  "dead" group have outside libs, or do my inside stones share all their libs with it?

